L000156	\begin{abstract}
L000157	We report the decisive resolution of the ground state spin and parity of the proton-dripline nucleus $^{22}$Al, a prime candidate for a proton halo.
L000158	The resolution stems from the first $\beta$-delayed charged particle emission experiment in the Gas Stopping Area at the Facility for Rare Isotope Beams (FRIB), leveraging high-intensity, low-energy beams extracted from the Advanced Cryogenic Gas Stopper (ACGS).
L000159	The pristine beam quality from FRIB and the ACGS enabled a sensitive particle identification technique using thin silicon detectors, allowing for the suppression of the dominant proton background and the first observation of the weak $\beta$-delayed $\alpha$ transition from the Isobaric Analog State in $^{22}$Mg to the $^{18}$Ne ground state.
L000160	This observation uniquely fixes the $^{22}$Al ground state as $4^+$.
L000161	The valence proton is confined by a dominant $d$-wave centrifugal barrier which, combined with the Coulomb repulsion, hinders the tunneling required for halo formation despite the exceptionally low proton separation energy of $^{22}$Al.
L000162	\end{abstract}
L000163	
L000164	\maketitle
L000165	
L000166	
L000167	
L000168	
L000169	
L000170	Near the limits of nuclear stability, the classical picture of the nucleus as a compact liquid drop breaks down, giving rise to the nuclear ``halo''.
L000171	This exotic structure emerges in loosely bound systems where a vanishing separation energy allows valence nucleons to tunnel far into the classically forbidden region.
L000172	The resulting spatially extended wavefunction creates a unique state of dilute nuclear matter that defies standard geometric scaling laws, serving as a distinct testbed for quantum phenomena at the edge of unbinding.
L000173	
L000174	While weak binding is a necessary condition for halo formation, it is not sufficient; the quantum tunneling required to sustain a halo is critically sensitive to the confining potential barriers \cite{Rii94,Jen04}.
L000175	For a halo to develop, the valence nucleon must not be inhibited by a high potential barrier.
L000176	Consequently, the orbital angular momentum, $l$, plays a decisive role.
L000177	The centrifugal barrier, proportional to $l(l+1)$, strongly suppresses the tunneling of nucleons in high-$l$ orbitals, effectively confining the wavefunction to the nuclear core.
L000178	Therefore, halos are predominantly associated with $s$-waves ($l=0$) or occasionally $p$-waves ($l=1$).
L000179	This structural fragility is further compounded in proton-rich nuclei by the Coulomb barrier.
L000180	Unlike neutrons, which can form halos relatively easily in light nuclei (such as $^{11}\text{Li}$ or $^{11}\text{Be}$), loosely bound protons are contained by the repulsive long-range Coulomb potential, which tends to truncate the outer tail of the wavefunction.
L000181	As a result, genuine proton halos are rare and remain a subject of intense debate \cite{Tan96,Jon04,Leh22,Zha24,Zha25}.
L000182	
L000183	Proton halos in the $sd$-shell have been envisaged since \cite{Bro96}, often in light isotopes of Si and P.
L000184	Recently, much interest has been given to the lightest bound Al isotope, $^{22}\mathrm{Al}$.
L000185	The recent interest started with the observation \cite{Lee20} of an asymmetry between the $\beta$-decays into the lowest excited $1^+$ states in $^{22}\mathrm{Al}$ and its mirror nucleus $^{22}\mathrm{F}$ that was attributed to a proton halo structure.
L000186	Two detailed theory papers \cite{Zha24,Pap25} do not support this conclusion, in particular not for the ground state, but acknowledge the incomplete experimental situation that has been partially alleviated by recent mass measurements \cite{Cam24,Sun24} which have established the proton separation energy of $^{22}\mathrm{Al}$ to be $S_p = 100.3(8)$ keV
L000187	\footnote{The proton separation energy $S_p = 100.3(8)$ keV of $^{22}\mathrm{Al}$ is the weighted average of 100.4(8) keV from \cite{Cam24} and 90(10) keV from \cite{Sun24}.}.
L000188	
L000189	The realization of a halo structure depends entirely on the quantum numbers of the valence nucleon, which dictate the height of the centrifugal barrier.
L000190	The structure of $^{22}\text{Al}$ can be modeled as a valence proton coupled to a $^{21}\text{Mg}$($5/2$) core, but the spin and parity, $J^\pi$, of the $^{22}\text{Al}$ ground state have remained ambiguous, with experimental evidence and theoretical models oscillating between a $3^+$ and a $4^+$ assignment, as discussed below.
L000191	This distinction is the primary determining factor for the existence of a halo.
L000192	A $J^\pi = 3^+$ assignment may allow the valence proton to occupy an $s_{1/2}$ orbital ($l=0$) relative to the core, facing no centrifugal barrier and allowing for the possible formation of an extended proton halo.
L000193	Conversely, a $J^\pi = 4^+$ assignment places the proton in a $d_{5/2}$ orbital ($l=2$), where the combined centrifugal and Coulomb barriers would confine the proton, resulting in a more standard nuclear structure.
L000194	
L000195	
L000196	
L000197	The structural interpretation of \alum{} is informed by knowledge from its mirror nucleus, \flor{}, which decays to the stable and well-characterized \cite{Bas15} nuclide $^{22}$Ne.
L000198	Together with \magn{} and $^{22}$Na, these nuclides constitute an isospin $T=2$ quintet.
L000199	While the $\beta$-decay strength from \flor{} to excited states in $^{22}$Ne restricts the \flor{} ground state to an even parity and a spin of $J=3$ or $4$ \cite{Dav74}, a precise assignment remains elusive.
L000200	Conflicting interpretations arise from reaction studies populating the analog 5.523 MeV state in $^{22}$Ne:
L000201	$^{18}$O($^{6}$Li,d) and $^{20}$Ne(t,p) favor $J=4$ \cite{Ana77,Mao94}, while $^{13}$C($^{11}$B,d) favors $J=3$ \cite{Sza83}.
L000202	
L000203	Under strict isospin symmetry, the ground state of \alum{} shares the tentative $J^{\pi}=(3,4)^+$ assignment of \flor{}.
L000204	However, the level structure is complicated by the presence of a first excited state in \flor{} at just 72 keV.
L000205	The de-excitation of this state to the ground state is found in \cite{Lee07} to change the spin by one unit and is assigned $3^+$, assuming the ground state of \flor{} is $4^+$.
L000206	What is clear, based on the available literature on the $T=2$ quintet, is that the two lowest-lying states in \flor{} are $3^+$ and $4^+$; two $3^+$ states in such close proximity is highly unlikely.
L000207	It is, however, unclear which of the two states has the lowest energy in \flor{}.
L000208	In the proton-rich \alum{}, the Thomas-Ehrman shift—driven by the spatial extension of the unbound proton wavefunction—could lower the energy of the analog $3^+$ state, which is associated with a low angular momentum $l$.
L000209	This effect could conceivably induce an inversion in the ground and first excited states in \alum{} relative to \flor{}, if the first excited state in \flor{} is indeed a $3^+$ state.
L000210	In other words, the ground state of \alum{} could be a $3^+$ state regardless of the ground state of \flor{} possibly being a $4^+$ state.
L000211	
L000212	
L000213	
L000214	We report the first observation of $\beta$-delayed $\alpha$ emission from the \alum{} ground state, proceeding via the Isobaric Analog State (IAS) in \magn{} to the ground state of \neon{}.
L000215	As detailed below, this observation (1) uniquely determines the spin and parity of the \alum{} ground state to be $4^+$, (2) resolves the long-standing ambiguity in the $A=22$ isospin quintet and (3) challenges the status of \alum{} as a halo nucleus.
L000216	
L000217	\begin{figure}
L000218	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{decay-scheme_mod.pdf}
L000219	\caption{\label{fig:decay-scheme}
L000220	Decay scheme for the $\beta$-delayed $\alpha$ emission of $^{22}$Al.
L000221	The newly observed transition to the $^{18}$Ne ground state is highlighted in red.
L000222	The mass and proton separation energy of \alum{} are adopted from \cite{Cam24}, and the IAS energy is derived from our measured $\beta$-delayed proton spectrum.
L000223	Other level parameters are adopted from \cite{Til95,Lee20,Fir15}.
L000224	The 1.01 MeV $1^+$ state in $^{22}$Al is the lowest-lying known excited state, and is particle-unbound.
L000225	Its energy is recalculated, based on the new mass measurement in \cite{Cam24}, increasing it from 0.91 MeV reported in \cite{Lee20}.
L000226	}
L000227	\end{figure}
L000228	
L000229	The relevant decay scheme is presented in Fig. \ref{fig:decay-scheme}.
L000230	Previous studies at GANIL \cite{Bla97,Ach06} and HIRFL \cite{Wu21} identified the $\beta$-delayed $\alpha$ branch to the first excited ($2^+$) state of \neon{} (indicated by the blue arrow).
L000231	However, the critical transition to the $0^+$ ground state (indicated by the red arrow) remained unobserved in these experiments, likely due to the overwhelming background from $\beta$-delayed protons.
L000232	
L000233	The present measurement was performed at the Facility for Rare Isotope Beams (FRIB).
L000234	A 5 kW primary beam of $^{36}$Ar was accelerated to 210 MeV/u before impinging on a 8.07 mm carbon production target.
L000235	The resulting in-flight cocktail beam was momentum-to-charge-separated in the Advanced Rare Isotope Separator (ARIS) \cite{Por23,Fuk23}, reducing the beam energy to 106 MeV/u.
L000236	Subsequently, the beam was guided to the Gas Stopping Area for further momentum compression, thermalization and purification.
L000237	A pure, low-energy 30 keV beam of \alum{} was extracted from the Advanced Cryogenic Gas Stopper (ACGS) \cite{Lun20,Rin21} and implanted into a thin carbon foil surrounded by a compact array of silicon detector telescopes.
L000238	Just outside of the vacuum chamber, containing the silicon detector telescopes, were two High-Purity Germanium detectors from the LIBRA setup \cite{Sun25}, flanking the chamber.
L000239	The low beam energy and the use of thin (60--70 µm) $\Delta E$ detectors allowed the dominant high-energy protons above 2--3 MeV to punch through the thin detectors, depositing only a fraction of their energy, while fully stopping $\alpha$ particles up to $\sim$9 MeV \cite{Zie10}.
L000240	This suppression of the proton background, crucial for isolating the rare $\alpha$ decay channels, was made possible by the characteristics of the low energy beams extracted from the ACGS.
L000241	
L000242	\begin{figure}
L000243	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{spectra_mod.pdf}
L000244	\caption{\label{fig:spectra}
L000245	\textbf{Top:} Singles spectrum of $\alpha$-particle kinetic energies, $E_\alpha$, extracted from the $\Delta E$ detectors.
L000246	The labels $\alpha_0$ and $\alpha_1$ denote transitions from the IAS in \magn{} to the $0^+$ ground and $2^+$ first excited states of \neon{}, respectively.
L000247	The background spectrum (scaled from 25 h to the 33 h \alum{} measurement time) is dominated by pre-implanted calibration sources.
L000248	The inset shows coincidences with the 1.887 MeV $\gamma$-transition in \neon{}.
L000249	\textbf{Bottom:} \neon{} recoil energies, $E_{^{18}\mathrm{Ne}}$, in coincidence with $\alpha$ particles in opposing silicon detectors.
L000250	The $\alpha$ particles are found above the proton punch through thresholds in the $\Delta E$ detectors (as in the singles spectrum), and any signal in opposing detectors is considered a recoil.
L000251	}
L000252	\end{figure}
L000253	
L000254	The upper panel of Fig. \ref{fig:spectra} displays the energy spectrum of $\alpha$ particles stopped in the $\Delta E$ detectors.
L000255	Two distinct peaks associated with the decay of \alum{} are visible above the background.
L000256	The background arises from long-lived calibration sources implanted prior to the experiment.
L000257	The peak at 3.29 MeV ($\alpha_1$) corresponds to the previously known transition to the $^{18}$Ne($2^+$) state.
L000258	The newly observed peak at 4.83 MeV ($\alpha_0$) corresponds to the transition to the $^{18}$Ne($0^+$) ground state.
L000259	
L000260	The identification is supported by coincidence measurements.
L000261	The lower panel of Fig. \ref{fig:spectra} shows $^{18}$Ne-$\alpha$ coincidences demonstrating the detection of low-energy signals from the opposing silicon detectors, corresponding to $^{18}$Ne nuclei recoiling with the expected kinetic energies of 1.07 MeV and 0.73 MeV for the ground and excited state transitions, respectively.
L000262	We emphasize that the pure, low-energy beam extracted from the ACGS enabled the measurement of the low-energy \neon{} recoils.
L000263	Furthermore, a gate on the 1.887 MeV $\gamma$-ray line (the $2^+ \to 0^+$ transition in \neon{}) isolates the $\alpha_1$ branch, as shown in the inset of the upper panel.
L000264	The particle energies in Fig. \ref{fig:spectra} have been corrected for energy losses specific to $\alpha$ particles in the detector dead layers and for the pulse height defect (PHD) inherent to silicon detectors calibrated with protons.
L000265	The observed broadening of the recoil energies is consistent with the broader distribution of energy losses of the heavy ions in the dead layers and with PHD uncertainties.
L000266	
L000267	\begin{table}
L000268	\caption{\label{tab:br-penet}
L000269	Kinetic energies, $E_\alpha$, relative branching ratios, $BR$, and calculated single-particle penetrabilities, $P_l$, for $\alpha$ emission from the \magn{} IAS with orbital angular momentum $l$.
L000270	Systematic energy uncertainties ($\sim$30 keV) dominate over statistical errors. $P_l$ values are median results from a uniform sampling of channel radii $R = r_0(A^{1/3}_1 + A^{1/3}_2) \, \mathrm{fm}$ with $r_0 \in [1.2, 1.4]$ and $A_1=18, A_2=4$.
L000271	}
L000272	\begin{ruledtabular}
L000273	\begin{tabular}{ r | c c }
L000274	                 & $\alpha_0 \, (l=4) \rightarrow \,^{18}\textrm{Ne}(0^+)$   & $\alpha_1 \, (l=2) \rightarrow \,^{18}\textrm{Ne}(2^+)$ \\
L000275	\colrule
L000276	$E_\alpha$ (MeV) & 4.83                                                      & 3.29                                                    \\
L000277	$BR$ (\%)        & 22(2)                                                     & 78(2)                                                   \\
L000278	$P_l$            & 0.44(9)                                                   & 0.33(6)
L000279	\end{tabular}
L000280	\end{ruledtabular}
L000281	\end{table}
L000282	
L000283	
L000284	The relative branching ratios are summarized in Table \ref{tab:br-penet}. The transition to the $2^+$ state is favored, 78\%, over the ground state, 22\%, despite the latter having a slightly larger barrier penetrability.
L000285	The ratio of the observed branching ratios, ${BR}_{\alpha_0}/{BR}_{\alpha_1} \approx 0.28$, contrasts with the ratio of penetrabilities, $P_{\alpha_0}/P_{\alpha_1} \approx 1.3$, indicating a hindrance of the ground state transition by a factor of roughly 4--5.
L000286	
L000287	The observation of the $\alpha_0$ branch allows for a definitive assignment of the \alum{} ground state spin and parity:
L000288	The emission of an $\alpha$ particle to a $0^+$ state requires, by conservation of angular momentum and parity, that the parent state has natural parity and $J^\pi = l^\pi$.
L000289	Since the IAS in \magn{} is populated via superallowed Fermi decay, it shares the spin and parity of the \alum{} ground state.
L000290	A $J^\pi = 3^+$ assignment would require $l=3$ for decay to a $0^+$ daughter, which implies negative parity, leading to a violation of parity conservation.
L000291	Therefore, the observation of the $\alpha_0$ branch mandates that the IAS—and consequently the ground state of \alum{}—is $4^+$.
L000292	
L000293	
L000294	
L000295	The newly determined proton separation energy of \alum{}, $S_p = 100.3(8)$ keV \cite{Cam24,Sun24}, is exceptionally low and comparable to the archetype proton halo nucleus $^{8}$B \cite{Sme99,Kor18} with $S_p = 136(1)$ keV.
L000296	This proximity to the dripline has fueled speculation that \alum{} might exhibit a proton halo.
L000297	However, halo formation is not determined solely by binding energy; it requires a structural configuration that allows the valence nucleon to tunnel through the confining potentials.
L000298	
L000299	If the ground state of \alum{} were $3^+$, the valence proton could occupy an $s_{1/2}$ ($l=0$) orbital coupled to the $^{21}$Mg($5/2^+$) core ground state.
L000300	Lacking a centrifugal barrier, such a configuration would favor the formation of an extended halo.
L000301	In contrast, the $4^+$ assignment mandates that a proton coupled to the $^{21}$Mg ground state must occupy a $d_{5/2}$ ($l=2$) orbital.
L000302	An $s$-wave component in the $4^+$ state is only possible via coupling to high-lying excited states of the core (e.g., $7/2^+, 9/2^+$), a configuration that is energetically suppressed.
L000303	
L000304	
L000305	
L000306	
L000307	
L000308	
L000309	
L000310	
L000311	
L000312	
L000313	
L000314	
L000315	
L000316	
L000317	
L000318	
L000319	
L000320	
L000321	
L000322	This qualitative picture is supported by recent microscopic calculations.
L000323	Relativistic Hartree-Bogoliubov studies treating \alum{} as a deformed or triaxial system \cite{Zha24,Pap25} indicate that even under favorable conditions, the ground state wavefunction is dominated ($>90\%$) by the $d$-wave component.
L000324	Consequently, these models predict no significant enhancement of the root-mean-square radius relative to neighboring isotopes.
L000325	Furthermore, recent \emph{ab-initio} calculations \cite{Li23} suggest that sizable Thomas-Ehrman shifts are ubiquitous in $sd$-shell mirror nuclei; these shifts can rationalize the low separation energy and level ordering in the $A=22$ system without necessitating the spatial delocalization characteristic of a halo.
L000326	
L000327	We therefore conclude that despite the vanishingly small separation energy, the $4^+$ ground state of \alum{} is likely a standard nuclear system confined by high potential barriers.
L000328	However, we note that a subtle enhancement of the surface density or a "soft" tail in the wavefunction cannot be strictly excluded by spectroscopy alone.
L000329	The ultimate confirmation of the compact nature of \alum{} awaits a direct measurement of its charge radius.
L000330	
L000331	
L000332	
L000333	The successful delivery of exotic nuclei at low energy from the ACGS at FRIB represents a significant advance for experimental studies near the driplines.
L000334	In this work, the pure, low-energy beams from the ACGS enabled both a sensitive particle identification technique that separated rare low-energy $\alpha$ particles from the dominant proton background and the detection of coincident low-energy \neon{} recoils.
L000335	This capability was instrumental in the first observation of the $\beta$-delayed $\alpha$ decay to the ground state of \neon{}.
L000336	
L000337	This observation has immediate and decisive consequences for the structure of \alum{}.
L000338	By unambiguously assigning a spin and parity of $4^+$ to the ground state, we have ruled out the $s$-wave coupling required for a pronounced proton halo.
L000339	Instead, the valence proton is confined by a $d$-wave centrifugal barrier which, in concert with the Coulomb barrier, renders the formation of a halo highly improbable despite the vanishingly small proton separation energy.
L000340	
L000341	Furthermore, the assignment of $4^+$ to the \alum{} ground state has implications for the low-energy structure of \flor{} with its first excited state located just 72 keV above its ground state.
L000342	These two states are $3^+$ and $4^+$, but their ordering is unclear, as explained above.
L000343	Our result does not uniquely settle this, but strongly favors $4^+$ also for the \flor{} ground state.
L000344	
L000345	The lowest-lying known excited $1^+$ state in \alum{} (Fig. \ref{fig:decay-scheme}) is unbound and thus cannot form a halo \cite{Zha24}.
L000346	Should an excited $3^+$ state in \alum{} lie below the proton separation energy $S_p = 100.3(8)$ keV, it remains a candidate for extended structure.
L000347	
L000348	Finally, while our spectroscopic result provides the angular momentum constraint deemed critical in \cite{Cam24}, the ultimate quantification of the proton wavefunction's spatial extent requires a direct observable. We advocate for a charge radius measurement as the final arbiter.
L000349	While interaction cross-section measurements could provide complementary evidence, laser spectroscopy of low-energy beams—now feasible in the FRIB Gas Stopping Area—appears to be the most direct path to conclusively settle the question of the \alum{} radius.
